    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Daoism grew from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/daodejing">Daodejing</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhuangzi">Zhuangzi</a>, works of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warring-states-period">Warring States period</a> which taught harmony with the Dao, the Way underlying all things, through simplicity, spontaneity and effortless action.</li><li>From 142 CE, with the Way of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/way-of-the-five-pecks-of-rice">Celestial Masters</a>, it became an organised religion of priests, rituals, scriptures and gods, seeking long life and transcendence, and emperors, above all the Tang, who claimed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/laozi">Laozi</a> as their ancestor, patronised it.</li><li>It shaped Chinese medicine, painting, poetry, alchemy and the martial arts, survived the persecutions of the 20th century, and its classic, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/daodejing">Daodejing</a>, is one of the most translated works in world literature.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>The earliest known description of a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gunpowder">gunpowder</a>-like mixture comes from a Daoist alchemical text of about the mid-9th century, which warns that heating saltpetre and sulphur with honey has burnt alchemists’ hands and faces and even their houses.</p></aside>
    </section>

